From 5ca9c6a25d2a5cf5455f1befd8e0a93a70709a6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius Gehrmann <marius@gehrmann.solutions>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:39:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] drm: apple: Follow the J613 lid: tell the panel's DCP,
 power the panel off

On J613 the internal panel's pipe can also drive a display on the
left-front USB-C port, but only once its panel is off: the pipe's first
DPTX core drives the panel otherwise. macOS tells the panel's IOMFB the
lid state and powers the panel off in clamshell mode. Once told the lid
is closed, the firmware takes the pipe for a second external display at
every panel power-on and swallows each panel frame ("timings are not
enabled for second external dispay on internal pipe"); a swallowed frame
never completes, the swap times out and the DCP is gone until reboot.

Do as macOS for the internal pipe that has a Type-C route
(dcp->clamshell), and treat a closed lid as a panel that is not there:

- Follow the lid switch (SW_LID, the SMC's on Apple laptops) with an
  input handler, registered once the panel has started.
- Tell IOMFB the state with A475 (one u32: 1 open, 2 closed): the real
  state from the start, then every change. IOMFB hands it to its DPTX
  power controller at each panel power-on, which refuses anything else.
- Closing the lid powers the panel off at once (A472(0)), its CRTC on or
  not, after a black frame while IOMFB still has the lid open. From
  A475(2) on no frame goes to the panel; one that raced with the lid is
  dropped under the session lock, not sent.
- While the lid is closed the panel's connector reads disconnected, from
  boot on if the lid is closed then, so compositors switch its CRTC off
  and never light it. A CRTC switched on anyway leaves the panel off and
  gets no frames (one warning).
- When the lid opens IOMFB is told first (A475(1)), then a panel whose
  CRTC is on is powered on again (A472(1), colour matrix resent), and
  its connector reads connected again.
- Answer what the firmware sends around those power changes: D2, D591
  and D574 as on an external processor, D593 (four bytes once after
  power-on, meaning not known) with nothing.

Other machines, and J613 without the Type-C route, are unchanged. The
next patch lets the free pipe drive the display on its route.

